  "source_notes": "Extracted from everlywell.com product pages, 2026-07-13, via WebFetch on each /products/<slug>/ page found in the ecommerce sitemap (https://www.everlywell.com/ecommerce-sitemap.xml). Everlywell 360 confirmed unchanged at $399/83 biomarkers (matches pre-migration DB exactly, no drift this time). The pre-migration DB's second row, 'Everlywell+ (general membership)' ($39/mo, 1 free test/month + 20% off), is a discount club layered on top of the à la carte catalog, not a real bundle -- excluded entirely, same treatment as Marek's/Vitals Vault's checkout-only ambiguous add-ons. Excluded 'womens-health-test-landing-page' as a duplicate marketing page for 'womens-health-test' (same product, confirmed by URL naming). Merged 'mens-health-test' and 'mens-testosterone-performance-test' into one product -- both are $249 with the exact same 4-marker list (Cortisol, DHEA, Estradiol, Total Testosterone), confirmed identical via direct comparison, kept the 'Men's Health Test' title as canonical. All other apparently-overlapping pairs (chlamydia-gonorrhea-test $69 at-home vs. chlamydia-gonorrhea-in-person-test $94 in-person; std-in-person-test-basic $119 vs. at-home-std-test $169 vs. visby-sexual-health-test $149) were verified via each page's own content to be genuinely different products (different pathogen coverage and/or collection method/turnaround), so all kept as separate SKUs. Everlywell also sells non-blood-biomarker telehealth/prescription items (e.g. acyclovir for cold sores) which are entirely excluded -- not part of the blood-test business this migration covers. Three products (Food Sensitivity Comprehensive, Jona Gut Microbiome, Galleri) have no itemizable individual biomarker list on their public pages (204 unlisted foods, 170,000+ unlisted microorganisms, and a proprietary DNA methylation signature respectively) -- represented with a single summary raw-marker string each per their own page description, since none would ever match our tracked blood-chemistry/hormone biomarkers table regardless of itemization. Reclassified three products from the initially-assumed category based on actual page content: Celiac Disease Screening (2 markers) and FIT Colon Cancer Screening (1 marker) are ala-carte-shaped despite being screening 'tests' in name, and Women's Fertility Test / Heart Health Test are ala-carte (single-focus, few-marker tests) rather than broad panels. All infectious-disease/STD tests are ala-carte with each screened pathogen listed as its own raw marker string (decomposed, same convention as Marek's multi-component combo tests).",
